 "bloques": [
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": {
    "@es": "De dónde eres",
    "@en": "Where you from"
   },
   "texto": [
    {
     "@es": "**Where are you from?** es «¿de dónde eres?». Fíjate en que **from** (de) va al final.",
     "@en": "**Where are you from?** is «Where are you from?». See that **from** (de) goes to the end."
    },
    {
     "@es": "Se responde **I'm from Spain** (soy de España) o con la nacionalidad: **I'm Spanish** (soy español).",
     "@en": "**I'm from Spain** (I am from Spain) or with nationality: **I'm Spanish** (I am Spanish)."
    },
    {
     "@es": "Países, nacionalidades e idiomas se escriben **siempre con mayúscula**: Spain, Spanish, English.",
     "@en": "Countries, nationalities and languages are written **siempre con mayúscula**: Spain, Spanish, English."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": {
    "@es": "Negar y preguntar con «to be»",
    "@en": "Deny and ask with «to be»"
   },
   "texto": [
    {
     "@es": "Para negar se añade **not**: **I'm not English**. Con he y she se usa **isn't** (is not); con you, **aren't** (are not).",
     "@en": "To deny, **not**: **I'm not English** is added. With he and she is used **isn't** (is not); with you, **aren't** (are not)."
    },
    {
     "@es": "Para preguntar, el verbo va delante: **Are you Spanish?**, **Is he from London?**",
     "@en": "To ask, the verb goes ahead: **Are you Spanish?**, **Is he from London?**"
    },
    {
     "@es": "Las respuestas cortas son muy comunes: **Yes, I am** / **No, I'm not** · **Yes, he is** / **No, he isn't**.",
     "@en": "Short responses are very common: **Yes, I am** / **No, I'm not** · **Yes, he is** / **No, he isn't**."
    }
   ]
  },
